concepts.body:
A link is an `<a>` element, and its `href` attribute says where it goes. Web addresses are called **URLs**, and `href` can hold several kinds:

- a full URL like `https://example.com/docs`, which works from anywhere;
- a path starting with `/`, like `/about`, which starts from the top folder of your own site;
- a **relative path** like `pricing.html` or `../index.html`, which starts from the folder of the current page (`../` means "go up one folder");
- `#` plus an id, like `#faq`, which scrolls to the element with `id="faq"` on the same page;
- special kinds such as `mailto:hi@example.com` (start an email) or `tel:+15551234` (start a phone call).

Write **link text** that makes sense on its own: "Read the pricing guide", not "click here". Screen-reader users often listen to a list of all links on a page, and search engines use the text to understand where the link goes.

`target="_blank"` opens the link in a new tab. Browsers make sure the new page can't take control of yours, but use it sparingly: people can open new tabs themselves. Add `rel="nofollow"` or `rel="sponsored"` to links you don't vouch for or were paid for, so search engines don't count them as recommendations.

The `download` attribute asks the browser to save the file instead of opening it (this only works for files on your own site).

A link takes you somewhere; a button does something on the current page. If clicking runs JavaScript and goes nowhere, use `<button>`, not `<a href="#">`. You may wrap a whole card — heading, picture and text — in one `<a>`, as long as there are no other links or buttons inside it.
